1️⃣ S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) 🎯

The rule: A class should have ONLY ONE reason to change. One class = one job. Period.

Real analogy: Imagine hiring ONE person to be your chef, waiter, AND accountant all at once 🤯. The moment the menu changes OR the billing system changes OR the serving style changes — this ONE overloaded person's job description changes for THREE unrelated reasons. Chaos. Instead, hire a chef, a waiter, and an accountant SEPARATELY — each has ONE clear job.

❌ BAD (violates SRP):

java
class User {
    String name;
    String email;

    void saveToDatabase() {
        // database logic here
        System.out.println("Saving user to DB... 💾");
    }

    void sendWelcomeEmail() {
        // email logic here
        System.out.println("Sending email... 📧");
    }
}

This User class has to change if the DATABASE logic changes, AND if the EMAIL logic changes, AND if user data itself changes. THREE reasons to change one class = red flag 🚩

✅ GOOD (follows SRP):

java
class User {
    String name;
    String email;
    // ONLY handles user data, nothing else
}

class UserRepository {
    void save(User user) {
        System.out.println("Saving user to DB... 💾");
    }
}

class EmailService {
    void sendWelcomeEmail(User user) {
        System.out.println("Sending email... 📧");
    }
}

Now each class has exactly ONE job. Database logic changes? Only touch UserRepository. Email logic changes? Only touch EmailService. User stays untouched. 🎯

🎤 One-liner: "A class should have only one reason to change — meaning it should do one job and do it well, rather than bundling unrelated responsibilities."

2️⃣ O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP) 🚪

The rule: Classes should be OPEN for extension, but CLOSED for modification. Meaning: you should be able to ADD new functionality WITHOUT changing existing, already-tested code.

Real analogy: Think of a power strip 🔌 vs rewiring your house's electrical system. Want to plug in a new device? Just plug it into the power strip (EXTEND) — you don't need to rip open the walls and rewire everything (MODIFY existing stuff). Good code should let you "plug in" new features the same way.

❌ BAD (violates OCP):

java
class DiscountCalculator {
    double calculateDiscount(String customerType, double amount) {
        if (customerType.equals("Regular")) {
            return amount * 0.05;
        } else if (customerType.equals("Premium")) {
            return amount * 0.10;
        }
        // 😱 every new customer type = editing this SAME method again
        return 0;
    }
}

Every time a new customer type shows up (VIP, Student, etc.), you have to go BACK into this method and add another if-else. You're MODIFYING already-working, already-tested code — risky, could break existing logic. 🚨

✅ GOOD (follows OCP):

java
interface DiscountStrategy {
    double calculate(double amount);
}

class RegularDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double calculate(double amount) { return amount * 0.05; }
}

class PremiumDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double calculate(double amount) { return amount * 0.10; }
}

// Want a NEW discount type? Just ADD a new class — never touch old ones!
class VIPDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double calculate(double amount) { return amount * 0.20; }
}

Adding VIPDiscount required creating a NEW class, not editing RegularDiscount or PremiumDiscount. Old, tested code stays untouched (CLOSED for modification), but the system grows (OPEN for extension). 🌱

🎤 One-liner: "Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification — you should be able to add new behavior without changing existing, tested code."

3️⃣ L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) 🔄

The rule: If class B is a subclass of class A, you should be able to REPLACE A with B ANYWHERE in your code WITHOUT breaking anything. Subclasses must behave consistently with what the parent PROMISES.

Real analogy: This is the FAMOUS "Square-Rectangle problem" 🟦. Mathematically, a Square IS a special Rectangle (all sides equal). Sounds logical to make Square extends Rectangle, right? But WATCH what happens...

❌ BAD (violates LSP):

java
class Rectangle {
    protected int width, height;

    void setWidth(int width) { this.width = width; }
    void setHeight(int height) { this.height = height; }
    int getArea() { return width * height; }
}

class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override
    void setWidth(int width) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = width; // 😱 forced to keep both sides equal
    }
    @Override
    void setHeight(int height) {
        this.width = height;
        this.height = height; // 😱 same forcing here
    }
}

// Somewhere else in your code, expecting normal Rectangle behavior:
Rectangle rect = new Square();
rect.setWidth(5);
rect.setHeight(10);
System.out.println(rect.getArea()); // Expected 50, but got 100! 🤯 BROKEN!

See the disaster? 💥 Code written to work with ANY Rectangle breaks silently when you sneak in a Square, because Square secretly changes behavior in a way the parent Rectangle never promised. This VIOLATES LSP — Square is NOT truly substitutable for Rectangle without breaking expectations.

✅ GOOD (follows LSP): Don't force an inheritance relationship that doesn't behaviorally hold. Instead:

java
interface Shape {
    int getArea();
}

class Rectangle implements Shape {
    int width, height;
    Rectangle(int w, int h) { width = w; height = h; }
    public int getArea() { return width * height; }
}

class Square implements Shape {
    int side;
    Square(int side) { this.side = side; }
    public int getArea() { return side * side; }
}

Now Square and Rectangle are SEPARATE implementations of a Shape interface — no broken inheritance promises, no surprise behavior. Any code using Shape works correctly regardless of which shape it actually gets. ✅

🎤 One-liner: "Objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of a subclass without altering the correctness of the program — a subclass shouldn't change the expected behavior of its parent."

4️⃣ I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) ✂️

The rule: Don't force a class to implement methods it doesn't actually need. Prefer many SMALL, specific interfaces over one giant "do everything" interface.

Real analogy: Imagine a job application form that asks EVERYONE — even a Software Engineer applicant — to fill in "Do you know how to fly a plane? ✈️" and "Rate your cooking skills 🍳" because the form was designed for ALL possible jobs combined. Ridiculous, right? Different roles need DIFFERENT, SPECIFIC forms — not one giant bloated form everyone's forced to fill entirely.

❌ BAD (violates ISP):

java
interface Worker {
    void code();
    void cook();
    void fly();
}

class SoftwareEngineer implements Worker {
    public void code() { System.out.println("Writing code... 💻"); }
    public void cook() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); } // 😬 forced to implement, makes no sense
    public void fly() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }  // 😬 same problem
}

SoftwareEngineer is FORCED to implement cook() and fly() even though it makes zero sense — pure interface bloat. 🚩

✅ GOOD (follows ISP):

java
interface Coder {
    void code();
}
interface Cook {
    void cook();
}
interface Pilot {
    void fly();
}

class SoftwareEngineer implements Coder {
    public void code() { System.out.println("Writing code... 💻"); }
    // only implements what it ACTUALLY needs!
}

Now SoftwareEngineer only implements Coder — no fake, meaningless methods forced onto it. Each interface is small and focused. 🎯

🎤 One-liner: "Clients shouldn't be forced to depend on methods they don't use — prefer multiple small, specific interfaces over one large, general-purpose interface."

5️⃣ D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) 🔌

The rule: High-level modules shouldn't depend directly on low-level modules — BOTH should depend on ABSTRACTIONS (interfaces). Also: depend on WHAT something does, not HOW it does it.

Real analogy: Think about a wall socket 🔌 in your house. Your lamp doesn't care if electricity comes from solar panels, a generator, or the government power grid — it just plugs into the STANDARD socket interface. The lamp depends on the "socket abstraction," NOT on the SPECIFIC power source. You could swap solar → generator anytime, and your lamp still works, unchanged.

❌ BAD (violates DIP):

java
class MySQLDatabase {
    void save(String data) {
        System.out.println("Saving to MySQL... 🗄️");
    }
}

class UserService {
    private MySQLDatabase database = new MySQLDatabase(); // 😬 directly depends on ONE specific database

    void saveUser(String data) {
        database.save(data);
    }
}

UserService is TIGHTLY coupled to MySQLDatabase specifically. Want to switch to MongoDB later (like you actually did across your projects)? You'd have to go EDIT UserService directly. Painful, risky. 🚨